<Base lang="en" title="Writing"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>The words arrive before the biography.</h1><p>Fiction, essays, and fragments that need not justify themselves at once live here. Chinese is the natural home of the originals; English pages carry context and a small number of carefully selected translations — not hurried machine versions of every piece.</p><div class="grid"><section class="card"><div><span class="number">ESSAYS</span><h3>100 Small Things About Toronto</h3><p>A published essay collection: one hundred small entries plus a set of Montreal notes, written with no outline — the recurring threads only became visible in hindsight.</p></div></section><section class="card"><div><span class="number">FANFICTION</span><h3>Fan fiction</h3><p>Mostly Genshin Impact, centered on one pairing across roughly thirty pieces, plus a handful of short pieces for other works. Someone else's story, carrying my own real thinking underneath.</p></div></section></div><div class="excerpts"><blockquote><p>Twenty dollars for a small box of tiramisu. That price was enough for a chicken and rice meal from the Kabab shop behind the school, a meal that could last me for two. My "broke international student" DNA screamed a piercing alarm at that moment... It was then that I understood. Graduation might not just mean starting to look for a job and pay rent. It also means you can finally, occasionally, willingly pay for something "unnecessary" but beautiful.</p><cite>Tiramisu Made of Gold</cite></blockquote><blockquote><p>A footlong. That's 30.48 centimeters! It was almost longer than my forearm! ...I needed both hands just to hold the sandwich, feeling like a hamster clutching a giant ear of corn. From then on, whenever I saw a coupon that said "Free," I would shudder. Because you never know what a heavy, sweet burden is hidden behind that "free" offer.</p><cite>The Sandwich Longer Than My Arm</cite></blockquote><blockquote><p>The word "free," like a bolt of lightning, struck me right in the heart... It was then that I understood that the saying my parents taught me since I was a child, "There's no such thing as a free lunch," didn't hold true at this magical Canadian university.</p><cite>Turns Out There Is Such a Thing as a Free Lunch</cite></blockquote><blockquote><p>One quiet, one lively; one refined, one hearty. These two little shops were like two silent guardians, holding open a small patch of breathing room — full of warmth and real flavor — for international students surrounded by fast food chains.</p><cite>My Taste Buds Are Saved!</cite></blockquote></div><aside class="note">Private files and unselected drafts are never synced here, and nothing is published without being cleared first.</aside></article></Base>

